7:T4e2,Attrapadung14 extracts the algebraic essence of "dual-system encryption for ABE" into a compact combinatorial object: a pair encoding scheme (PES) consisting of two polynomial encodings \[ c(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{b}) \text{ (ciphertext-side)} \qquad\text{and}\qquad k(\mathbf{r}, \boldsymbol{\alpha}, \mathbf{b}) \text{ (key-side)} \] each linear in its own randomness, with a decryption relation recovering \( \alpha\cdot s \) when the predicate holds. Security of the encoding is defined selectively and co-selectively (hence "doubly selective"); the paper's main theorem shows that a doubly-selective PES lifts to an adaptively secure ABE via dual-system encryption, in composite-order bilinear groups. This is the scaffolding paper for essentially every modern ABE. Specific PES designs in the paper yield first-of-their-kind constructions: KP-ABE for LSSS (information-theoretic PES, static subgroup decision). KP-ABE with short ciphertext (computational PES, q-type). ← this spec. KP-ABE with short secret key (computational PES; short-SK at cost of CT blowup). KP-ABE for regular languages (first adaptive DFA-ABE; computational PES). Unbounded KP-ABE for LSSS (computational PES). CP-ABE variants via the duality conversion (extended in AY15).8:T428,Type III bilinear group \( (\mathbb{G}_1, \mathbb{G}_2, \mathbb{G}_T, e) \), \( k \)-Lin distribution \( D_k \). Setup: sample small matrices \( A_1\in\mathbb{Z}_p^{(2k+1)\times k} \) (reduced from \( 3k \)), seed matrix \( B \) (height \( k+1 \)), a master secret \( \mathbf{k}\in\mathbb{Z}_p^{2k+1} \). Publish \( \mathrm{MPK} = ([A_1]_1, [A_1 W_j]_1\text{ for } O(1) \text{ fixed } W_j, [B\cdots]_1, [\mathbf{k}A_1]_T) \). KeyGen(\( (\mathbf{M},\rho) \)): for each LSSS row, produce \( 5k+3 \) \( \mathbb{G}_2 \) elements tying the row's label \( \rho(i)\in\mathbb{Z}_p \) to its expanded entropy \( s_{\rho(i)} \) derived from MSK. Encrypt(\( S, M \)): for each attribute \( y\in S \) build a ciphertext slot \( [c_y]_1 \) of \( 2k+1 \) \( \mathbb{G}_1 \) elements using entropy-expansion \( [W(y)\cdot\mathbf{s}]_1 \). Plus a header \( [A_1\mathbf{s}]_1 \) and masked \( M\cdot[\mathbf{k}A_1\mathbf{s}]_T \). Decrypt: reconstruction coefficients \( \omega_i \) for satisfying rows; one \( (2k+1) \)-dim multi-pairing per row → \( (2k+1)I \) scalar pairings.9:T414,LL20a extends KW19 from NC1 (monotone Boolean formulas) to the substantially more expressive class of arithmetic branching programs (ABPs) over \( \mathbb{Z}_p \), while preserving: (a) adaptive security, (b) compact (policy-size-independent) ciphertexts, (c) static \( k \)-Lin assumption, (d) polynomial security loss. The construction is also extended to give the first compact adaptive KP-ABE for DFA, NFA, logspace Turing machines, and NL, from the same \( k \)-Lin assumption — this is the "beyond NC1 toward NL" of the paper's subtitle. The backbone is a function-hiding slotted inner-product functional encryption (IPFE) (Appendix A, line going back to Lin 2017 / KLM+18) plus a novel arithmetic key garbling scheme (AKGS) that encodes \( \mu\cdot f(\mathbf{x}) \) with linear label functions satisfying a piecewise security property. Combining: (IPFE keys for per-vertex label functions) × (single IPFE ciphertext for attribute slots) → KP-ABE for ABPs with keys linear in \( |\mathrm{ABP}| \) and CT linear in \( |\mathbf{x}| \).a:T438,Setup(\( 1^n \)): run IPFE.Setup to get \( (\mathrm{msk}, \mathrm{mpk}) \) over \( \Theta(kn) \) public slots: \( \{\mathrm{pad}, \mathrm{const}^t, \mathrm{coef}_i^t\} \) for \( t\in[k], i\in[n] \). KeyGen(\( f \)): input ABP \( f:\mathbb{Z}_p^n\to\mathbb{Z}_p \). Run \( k \) garblings via AKGS to produce label functions \( L_1^t, \ldots, L_{m_{\text{ABP}}}^t \) for \( t\in[k] \). Output \( \mathrm{sk} = (\mathrm{isk}_{\mathrm{pad}}, f, \mathrm{isk}_1, \ldots, \mathrm{isk}_{m_{\text{ABP}}}) \) — \( (m_{\text{ABP}}+1) \) IPFE secret keys, each \( O(kn) \) group elements. Encrypt(\( \mathbf{x}, g \)): sample pad \( h, \mathbf{s}\in\mathbb{Z}_p^k \). Output \( \mathrm{ct} = (g + [h]_T, \mathbf{x}, \mathrm{ict}) \) where \( \mathrm{ict} \) is a single slotted IPFE ciphertext filling slots \( (\mathrm{pad} = h, \mathrm{const}^t = \mathbf{s}[t], \mathrm{coef}_i^t = \mathbf{s}[t]\mathbf{x}[i]) \). Decrypt: run IPFE.Dec on each \( \mathrm{isk}_j \) + \( \mathrm{ict} \) to get \( [\ell_j]_T \); evaluate AKGS linearly; subtract \( [h]_T \) from the tag to recover \( g \).b:T415,ABE-PAPER-2013-GVW and ABE-PAPER-2014-BGGPS gave circuit ABE of bounded depth
from LWE. ABE-PAPER-2023-HLL removed the depth bound, from circular security
for one key and from evasive circular LWE for unbounded collusion, and
ABE-PAPER-2025-AMY-TM obtained ciphertext-policy ABE of unbounded depth from
evasive and circular tensor LWE on the way to ABE for Turing machines.
ABE-PAPER-2024-AKY-PRFE removed the circular assumptions from both the
key-policy and the ciphertext-policy versions, still assuming private-coin
evasive LWE, and ABE-PAPER-2025-CW-TM gave faster ABE for Turing machines from
the circular evasive assumption of ABE-PAPER-2023-HLL.
ABE-PAPER-2025-AMYY-ZEROIZING attacked the evasive circular assumption and the
private-coin assumption of the first posting of ABE-PAPER-2024-AKY-PRFE in
2025, and ABE-PAPER-2026-AMYY proposed pairing-based replacements and stated
the remaining questions. ABE-PAPER-2026-AMR-DEPTH-UNBOUNDED then gave
ciphertext-policy ABE without a depth bound, for bounded space, from
decomposed LWE alone.c:T6cf,ABE-PAPER-2023-HLL constructs key-policy ABE for circuits of unbounded depth
and size, with constant-size secret keys, and proves it very selectively
secure under circular small-secret LWE and evasive circular small-secret LWE
(Theorem 23); the attribute-unbounded version and predicate encryption follow
under the same assumptions. Its evasive assumption is stated only for
public-coin samplers. ABE-PAPER-2025-AMY-TM constructs very selectively secure
ciphertext-policy ABE for circuits of unbounded depth and size from LWE, the
private-coin version of evasive LWE, and a new circular tensor LWE assumption
(Theorem 4.3). ABE-PAPER-2024-AKY-PRFE removes the circular assumptions: it
constructs very selectively secure key-policy and ciphertext-policy ABE for
circuits of unbounded depth, with public keys, keys and ciphertexts of size
\( \mathrm{poly}(\lambda) \), from LWE and private-coin evasive LWE alone
(Theorems 6.1 and 7.2). These sizes use an obfuscator for pseudorandom
functionalities from the companion paper ABE-PAPER-2024-AKY-PRMIFE; without
it, Theorem B.9 still gives key-policy ABE of unbounded depth from the same
assumptions, with longer keys. ABE-PAPER-2025-AMYY-ZEROIZING then attacked the
evasive circular LWE assumption of the first, by combining its circular
encodings with the key preimages, and the private-coin assumption of the first
posting of ABE-PAPER-2024-AKY-PRFE; the revision of that paper restricts the
admissible samplers and modifies its construction. ABE-PAPER-2026-AMYY reports
that private-coin evasive LWE was invalidated by a sequence of attacks, and
notes that the constructions remain unbroken. The theorems stand as
conditional statements, but they no longer rest on assumptions considered
safe.d:T467,The paper constructed the first ABE for arbitrary polynomial-size circuits from LWE and also exposed a connection to branching programs and LOGSPACE. Theorem 6.1 (§6, PDF p. 16) builds selectively secure key-policy ABE for circuits of depth at most \(d_{\max}\) on \(\ell\) input bits from any two-to-one recoding scheme correct for \(d_{\max}\) levels; Corollary 6.2 (PDF p. 16) instantiates it from decision LWE with modulus \(q=n^{O(d_{\max})}\) and polynomially bounded noise, which the informal Theorem 2.1 (PDF p. 3) states as LWE hard for an \(\exp(n^\epsilon)\) factor. In the selective game (§3.1, PDF p. 8) the adversary names the challenge index before setup and then makes key queries adaptively. Setup takes \(\ell\) and \(d_{\max}\); ciphertext size depends polynomially on \(d_{\max}\) and \(\ell\) but not on circuit size (§2.1, PDF p. 3), while a key holds four recoding keys per gate (§6.1, PDF p. 17). The informal Theorem 2.3 (PDF p. 3) and §7 give ABE for branching programs, with no bound on their size or depth, from LWE with an \(n^{\omega(1)}\) factor or from bilinear decisional Diffie–Hellman.e:T422,The work developed key-homomorphic encryption techniques leading to lattice ABE for arithmetic circuits with secret-key size governed by circuit depth rather than circuit size. Theorem 4.8 (§4.4, PDF p. 24) proves the key-policy ABE built from its fully key-homomorphic encryption correct and selectively secure under LWE, for every function family with key-homomorphic evaluation algorithms; §§4.1--4.3 (PDF pp. 19--23) supply them for arithmetic circuits, with noise growth exponential in the depth. For circuits of depth \(t(\lambda)\) the parameters take \(q=2^{n^\epsilon}\) and noise \(O(n)\) (§1, "Parameters and performance", PDF p. 6), and a secret key is a single \(2m\times m\) low-norm matrix, of size \(\operatorname{poly}(\lambda,d)\) (informal Theorem 1.1, PDF p. 2; "Secret key size", PDF p. 7). In the selective game (Definition 2.1, PDF p. 8) the adversary commits to the challenge attribute before setup. A second ABE, from multilinear maps, has ciphertexts of size \(|x|+\operatorname{poly}(\lambda,d)\) (Theorem 6.1, §6, PDF p. 31).f:T43d,Theorem 1 (PDF p. 9): from any Z_p-linear predicate encoding with the attribute-hiding and attribute-hiding-leakage-resilient properties of §3.1 (PDF p. 7), the construction of §3.2 (PDF pp. 7–8) is ABE in prime-order bilinear groups that is (0, L_sk)-bounded-leakage secure and attribute-hiding (Definition 1, PDF pp. 5–6) under the k-Lin assumption, with advantage at most (2q+1) times the k-Lin advantage plus a negligible term. Theorem 2 (PDF p. 14): a second framework (§4.1–4.2, PDF pp. 12–14), with separate redundant components in the master key and user keys and key-update algorithms, gives (L_mk, L_sk)-continual-leakage secure ABE (Definition 2, PDF p. 6) under k-Lin. It guarantees payload hiding only. The paper describes it as more generic than Zhang et al. (Designs, Codes and Cryptography 2018), because it applies to the compact-key inner-product schemes of ABE-PAPER-2015-CGW (§1.2, PDF p. 3). Section 5 (PDF pp. 14–15) instantiates the first framework for the zero inner-product predicate and the second for zero and non-zero inner-product predicates.10:T48c,The paper constructs compact functional encryption for pseudorandom functionalities from LWE and private-coin evasive LWE (Theorem 3.6, §3.3, PDF p. 36) and uses it for ABE. Theorem 6.1 (§6.1, PDF p. 67) gives very selectively secure KP-ABE for circuits of unbounded depth with \( |\mathrm{mpk}|, |\mathrm{sk}_C|, |\mathrm{ct}| = \mathrm{poly}(\lambda) \); Theorem 6.5 (PDF p. 69) gives KP-PE with ciphertexts \( \mathrm{poly}(\lambda) + |x| \); Theorem 7.2 (§7.1, PDF p. 71) gives CP-ABE with \( |\mathrm{mpk}|, |\mathrm{sk}_x|, |\mathrm{ct}| = \mathrm{poly}(\lambda) \); all under LWE and evasive LWE. Theorem B.9 (Appendix B.3, PDF p. 89) gives a non-optimal KP-ABE of unbounded depth from the same assumptions with \( |\mathrm{sk}_C| = |C| \cdot \ell \cdot \mathrm{poly}(\lambda) \), which the paper notes uses no circular assumption. Corollary 7.4 (PDF p. 73) instantiates the Turing-machine compiler of ABE-PAPER-2025-AMY-TM with these schemes to obtain very selectively secure ABE for Turing machines from LWE and evasive LWE, with \( |\mathrm{sk}| = |M| \cdot \mathrm{poly}(\lambda) \) and \( |\mathrm{ct}| = |x| \cdot t \cdot \mathrm{poly}(\lambda) \).11:T48b,The evasive LWE assumption is private-coin (Theorem 3.6) and is stated with respect to a sampler class (Assumption 2.7, PDF pp. 23–24); the authors conjecture that it holds for the samplers their proofs use. No circular assumption is used in Theorems 6.1, 6.5, 7.2 and B.9. Security is very selective: key queries and the challenge attribute are fixed before setup (Definition 2.15, PDF p. 27; Theorem 1.1 discussion, PDF p. 4). The optimal parameters of Theorems 6.1 and 7.2 use the polynomial-domain obfuscation for pseudorandom functionalities (pPRIO) of the companion paper ABE-PAPER-2024-AKY-PRMIFE (Theorem 2.32, PDF p. 31; §1.2, PDF p. 5); Theorem B.9 does not. Section 1.3 (PDF pp. 5–7) states that attacks by Agrawal–Modi–Yadav–Yamada and Hsieh–Jain–Lin, using a contrived PRF circuit inside the first posted construction, break the evasive LWE assumption for general samplers; the revision restricts the sampler class and adds a modulus-reduction step, and the authors know no malicious-sampler attack on the modified construction. Remarks 2.31 and 3.3 (PDF pp. 31–32) record that no pPRIO or pseudorandom FE is secure for all samplers.12:T4dd,The paper builds public-key ciphertext-policy ABE whose policies are Turing-machine computations with a space bound fixed at setup and no bound on their running time or depth (Theorem 1.1, p. 3; §3, pp. 10-15). A policy is a transition circuit \(C_f\) of depth \(O(\log d)\) on a \(d\)-bit state, iterated \(T\) times for any polynomial \(T\); keys carry attributes in \(\{0,1\}^d\), and setup takes only \(1^\lambda\) and \(1^d\) (§3.1, p. 10). The dimensions of the public key, keys and ciphertext components do not depend on \(T\); encryption takes time \( \mathrm{poly}(\lambda, d, |C_f|, \log T) \) and decryption \( \mathrm{poly}(\lambda, d, |C_f|, T) \) (§3.3, p. 12). Security is selective against any polynomial number of key queries (Definition 3.1, p. 10) under extended decomposed LWE (Theorem 3.3, p. 13), which the authors state is equivalent to ordinary decomposed LWE; decomposed LWE is a variant of LWE whose coefficient matrix has a public low-rank structure and is equivalent to Wee's succinct LWE under suitable parameters (§1.1, p. 2). The same technique gives attribute-based laconic function evaluation with hashing time polynomial in \( \log T \) (§3.5, p. 15) and lattice-based delay encryption (Theorem 1.2, p. 3).13:T529,Let \( \mathcal{G} \) output \( (N, \mathbb{G}, \mathbb{G}_T, e) \) where
\( N = p_1 p_2 \cdots p_n \) is a product of distinct primes,
\( \mathbb{G}, \mathbb{G}_T \) are cyclic of order \( N \), and
\( e : \mathbb{G} \times \mathbb{G} \to \mathbb{G}_T \) is bilinear. For
\( S \subseteq [n] \) let \( \mathbb{G}_S \) be the subgroup of order
\( \prod_{i \in S} p_i \).

- Subgroup decision (Boneh, Goh and Nissim 2005, \( n = 2 \)): given \( N \) and
  a generator, distinguish a random element of \( \mathbb{G}_{\{1\}} \) from a
  random element of \( \mathbb{G} \).
- General subgroup decision: distinguish a random element of
  \( \mathbb{G}_{S_0} \) from one of \( \mathbb{G}_{S_1} \), given random
  elements of further subgroups \( \mathbb{G}_{S_i} \) that intersect both or
  neither of \( S_0, S_1 \).

Dual-system proofs in composite-order groups (Lewko and Waters 2010, 2011)
combine instances of general subgroup decision with one further static
assumption of BDH type. In `ABE-PAPER-2011-LW-UNBOUNDED`, with
\( N = p_1 p_2 p_3 \), Assumptions 1, 3 and 4 are general subgroup decision
instances, while Assumption 2 asks to distinguish \( e(g,g)^{\alpha s} \) from
random given \( g, g_2, g_3, g^{\alpha} X_2, g^{s} Y_2 \) (with
\( X_2, Y_2 \in \mathbb{G}_{\{2\}} \)), which is not a subgroup decision
problem.14:T474,Let \( \mathbb{G} \) be a cyclic group of prime order \( p \). The DLIN
assumption states that, for uniform generators \( g_1, g_2, g_3 \) and uniform
exponents \( r_1, r_2 \in \mathbb{Z}_p \), no probabilistic polynomial-time
algorithm distinguishes

\[
(g_1, g_2, g_3, g_1^{r_1}, g_2^{r_2}, g_3^{r_1 + r_2}) \quad \text{from} \quad (g_1, g_2, g_3, g_1^{r_1}, g_2^{r_2}, Z)
\]

with \( Z \) uniform in \( \mathbb{G} \). It is the case \( k = 2 \) of k-Lin
(ASM-KLIN).

The same problem is used in two pairing settings:

- Type I (symmetric) pairings, as in Boneh, Boyen and Shacham (2004). Here DDH
  is easy, and DLIN is the natural replacement, holding in the generic
  bilinear group model.
- Type III (asymmetric) pairings, where the assumption is posed in
  \( \mathbb{G}_1 \) and in \( \mathbb{G}_2 \), as in FAME
  (`ABE-PAPER-2017-FAME`) and the DFA schemes of `ABE-PAPER-2019-AMY-DFA`. In
  Type III groups SXDH is believed to hold as well, and DLIN is the more
  conservative choice because SXDH implies it.

The problem is identical; what differs is the group setting and therefore
which stronger assumptions are also available.15:T570,Let \( B \leftarrow \mathbb{Z}_q^{n \times m} \) be uniform and let a sampler
output a matrix \( P \in \mathbb{Z}_q^{n \times t} \) and auxiliary input
\( \mathsf{aux} \). Write \( B^{-1}(P) \) for a matrix \( K \) with small
discrete Gaussian entries and \( B K = P \), sampled with a trapdoor for
\( B \). For a secret \( s \leftarrow \mathbb{Z}_q^{n} \) and errors
\( e, e' \), evasive LWE states that, for every efficient sampler in a
specified class, if

\[
(B, P, \mathsf{aux}, \; s B + e, \; s P + e') \approx_c (B, P, \mathsf{aux}, \; c, \; c')
\]

(the pre-condition, with \( c, c' \) uniform), then

\[
(B, P, \mathsf{aux}, \; s B + e, \; B^{-1}(P)) \approx_c (B, P, \mathsf{aux}, \; c, \; B^{-1}(P))
\]

(the post-condition). Informally, publishing short preimages of \( P \) helps
an adversary only through the products \( (sB + e) \cdot B^{-1}(P) \approx sP \),
which the pre-condition already makes pseudorandom. In the public-coin
variant the sampler's random coins are part of \( \mathsf{aux} \); in
private-coin variants they are hidden.

The assumption is a family indexed by samplers, and its pre-condition is
itself a computational indistinguishability statement. A claimed
counterexample must therefore establish a hardness claim, so the assumption is
not falsifiable in the usual sense, in contrast to succinct LWE
(ASM-SUCCINCT-LWE), which its author calls falsifiable.16:T5af,Wee (2022) and Tsabary (2022) introduced the assumption independently;
Vaikuntanathan, Wee and Wichs (2022) used it for witness encryption and null-iO and already noted that it
cannot hold for arbitrary auxiliary input, proposing restricted forms.
Counterexamples since then:

- Private-coin variants: Brzuska, Ünal and Woo (2024) gave LWE-based
  counterexamples to the private-coin variants used in several candidate
  constructions, and defined classes of variants that avoid them; Döttling,
  Jain, Malavolta, Mathialagan and Vaikuntanathan (2025) gave a direct
  zeroizing counterexample to all known private-coin variants; Hsieh, Jain and
  Lin (2025) and Agrawal, Modi, Yadav and Yamada (2025) gave zeroizing attacks
  on the private-coin assumptions behind proposed pseudorandom obfuscation and
  functional encryption schemes. Huang, Hung and Yamada (2026) showed that the
  2022 obfuscation-based attack does not work as stated, repaired it, and
  observed that private-coin evasive LWE restricted to samplers oblivious to
  the secret has no known attack.
- Public-coin variants: Agrawal, Modi, Yadav and Yamada (2025) refuted the
  circular evasive LWE assumption of `ABE-PAPER-2023-HLL` and gave a
  counterexample to Wee's public-coin evasive LWE in a regime where the
  pre-condition error is larger than the post-condition error. No
  counterexample to public-coin evasive LWE in its natural parameter regime is
  known as of 2026-10-10.17:T5b1,Let \( \mathbb{G} = \langle g \rangle \) have prime order \( p \) and write
\( [x] = g^{x} \) (implicit notation). For an integer \( k \ge 1 \), the k-Lin
assumption states that, for uniform \( a_1, \dots, a_k, r_1, \dots, r_k \in
\mathbb{Z}_p \), no probabilistic polynomial-time algorithm distinguishes

\[
\big([a_1], \dots, [a_k], [a_1 r_1], \dots, [a_k r_k], [r_1 + \cdots + r_k]\big)
\]

from the same tuple with the last entry replaced by a uniform element. In the
matrix Diffie-Hellman (MDDH) framework of Escala, Herold, Kiltz, Ràfols and
Villar (2013), this is \( \mathcal{L}_k \)-MDDH: \( ([A], [A w]) \approx
([A], [u]) \) where \( A \in \mathbb{Z}_p^{(k+1) \times k} \) has
\( a_1, \dots, a_k \) on the diagonal and a final row of ones,
\( w \leftarrow \mathbb{Z}_p^{k} \) and \( u \leftarrow \mathbb{Z}_p^{k+1} \).

Attribute-based encryption papers in this atlas write the assumption as
\( \mathrm{MDDH}_k \) or \( \mathrm{MDDH}_k^m \): the version with a uniformly random
\( (k+1) \times k \) matrix and \( m \) columns \( W \in \mathbb{Z}_p^{k \times m} \).
Every \( \mathcal{D}_k \)-MDDH assumption implies the uniform one
(Escala et al.), and the \( m \)-fold version follows from the single one by
random self-reducibility, so these papers state their theorems under k-Lin. In
pairing groups k-Lin is assumed in both \( \mathbb{G}_1 \) and
\( \mathbb{G}_2 \); \( k = 1 \) gives SXDH and \( k = 2 \) gives
DLIN in both groups (ASM-DLIN).18:T594,Regev (2005) gave a quantum reduction from the worst-case approximate lattice
problems GapSVP and SIVP to LWE with approximation factor
\( \tilde O(n/\alpha) \). Peikert (2009) gave a classical reduction from
GapSVP for exponential modulus \( q \ge 2^{n/2} \), and Brakerski, Langlois,
Peikert, Regev and Stehlé (2013) extended classical hardness to polynomial
modulus by trading dimension for modulus.

The best known attacks are lattice reduction (primal and dual attacks with
BKZ), combinatorial BKW-type algorithms, and hybrid attacks; all take time
\( 2^{\Theta(n)} \) for the parameters used in practice. Concrete security is
estimated with the lattice estimator introduced by Albrecht, Player and Scott
(2015) and its later versions; the authors of that paper note that its cost
models have since been superseded. Arora and Ge (2011) solve LWE in time
\( 2^{\tilde O(n^{2\varepsilon})} \) when \( \alpha q = n^{\varepsilon} \) with
\( \varepsilon < 1/2 \), and in polynomial time when the error takes only a few
known values and many samples are available; this rules out very narrow or
few-valued error distributions, not the regime with \( \alpha q > 2\sqrt n \).

In April 2024 Chen posted a claimed polynomial-time quantum algorithm for LWE
with certain polynomial approximation factors (ePrint 2024/555); on 18 April
2024 the author reported a bug in a step of the algorithm and stated that the
claim does not hold.19:T7e9,- **KP-ABE and CP-ABE**: in key-policy ABE the key carries the policy and the
  ciphertext carries attributes (ABE-PAPER-2006-GPSW); in ciphertext-policy ABE
  the roles are swapped (ABE-PAPER-2007-BSW).
- **Policy class**: threshold gates; Boolean formulas and monotone span
  programs, written as linear secret-sharing (LSSS) matrices; arithmetic
  branching programs (ABPs); \(\mathsf{NC}^1\); bounded-depth and
  unbounded-depth circuits; finite automata; Turing or RAM machines.
- **Collusion resistance**: holders of unauthorized keys cannot combine them to
  decrypt. Standard ABE requires this for an unbounded number of keys.
- **Selective, semi-adaptive, adaptive**: the adversary fixes the challenge
  attribute or policy before seeing the public key (selective), after the
  public key but before any key query (semi-adaptive), or at challenge time
  after adaptive key queries (adaptive, or full, security).
- **Universe and unboundedness**: a small-universe scheme fixes a
  polynomial-size attribute set at setup; a large-universe scheme accepts
  arbitrary strings. "Unbounded" can refer independently to the universe, the
  attribute length, the policy size, or the circuit depth. Multi-use schemes
  let one attribute label several policy rows.
- **Succinctness**: how public-key, secret-key, and ciphertext sizes grow with
  attribute length, policy size, and depth; "constant" means
  \(\mathrm{poly}(\lambda)\) in the security parameter \(\lambda\) only.
- **Model and assumption**: standard model, random-oracle model (ROM), or
  generic bilinear group model (GGM); static assumptions (k-Lin, LWE) versus
  q-type assumptions or strengthened LWE variants (succinct, evasive,
  circular).
- **Authority model**: one central authority; multi-authority ABE, where
  independent authorities issue keys for their own attributes
  (ABE-PAPER-2011-LW-MAABE); or registered ABE, where users generate their own
  keys and a transparent, secret-free aggregation step builds the public key
  (ABE-PAPER-2023-HLWW).1a:T523,- **Pairing-based secret sharing**: the master secret is shared along the
  policy and recovered in the target group. Expressive LSSS schemes are
  selective under q-type assumptions (ABE-PAPER-2011-WATERS);
  ABE-PAPER-2017-FAME and ABE-PAPER-2022-FABEO target concrete efficiency in
  the GGM with random oracles.
- **Dual system encryption**: semi-functional keys and ciphertexts give
  adaptive security under static assumptions, first in composite-order groups
  (ABE-PAPER-2010-LOSSTW), then through pair encodings
  (ABE-PAPER-2014-ATTRAPADUNG) and prime-order predicate encodings
  (ABE-PAPER-2015-CGW). ABE-PAPER-2019-KW gives adaptively secure \(\mathsf{NC}^1\) ABE
  from k-Lin.
- **Lattice key-homomorphic encodings**: bounded-depth circuit ABE from LWE
  (ABE-PAPER-2013-GVW, ABE-PAPER-2014-BGGPS), semi-adaptive with unbounded
  attribute length (ABE-PAPER-2016-BV), and adaptive for t-CNF policies with
  constant t (ABE-PAPER-2019-TSABARY). Succinct LWE gives short keys and
  ciphertexts together (ABE-PAPER-2024-WEE, ABE-PAPER-2025-WEE). Witness
  encryption gives a separate adaptive route (ABE-PAPER-2024-WW).
- **Reduced trust**: lattice multi-authority ABE (ABE-PAPER-2021-DKW,
  ABE-PAPER-2023-DKW) and registered ABE (ABE-PAPER-2024-GLWW,
  ABE-PAPER-2025-CHW-RABE, ABE-PAPER-2026-SWW-RABE).1b:T684,Size cells count group elements: `G1: 2*n1+1` means \(2n_1+1\) elements of
\(\mathbb{G}_1\). Generators are not counted.

| Symbol | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `m` | Size of the attribute set \(S\): in the key for CP-ABE, in the ciphertext for KP-ABE |
| `n1` | Number of rows of the LSSS policy matrix (leaves of the access tree in ABE-PAPER-2006-GPSW and ABE-PAPER-2007-BSW); the policy size |
| `I` | Number of satisfying rows (shares) used in decryption |
| `U` | Size of the attribute universe fixed at setup; for binary attribute vectors \(x\in\{0,1\}^n\) (ABE-PAPER-2019-KW, ABE-PAPER-2020-LL-EUROCRYPT, ABE-PAPER-2020-LL, ABE-PAPER-2022-LLL) it is the attribute length \(n\) |
| `tau` | Maximum number of policy rows labeled by one attribute (multi-use bound) |
| `k` | Parameter of the k-Lin (MDDH) assumption; \(k=1\) is SXDH |
| `Gamma` | Number of distinct attributes in the key policy (ABE-PAPER-2013-HW-FAST) |
| `T` | Bound on the ciphertext attribute-set size fixed at setup (ABE-PAPER-2014-ATTRAPADUNG) |
| `L` | Attribute-vector length in lattice circuit schemes |
| `d` | Circuit or formula depth bound fixed at setup |
| `mABP` | Number of vertices of the ABP policy |
| `nk`, `nc`, `mkey`, `mct` | Key and ciphertext partition sizes chosen at setup, and the resulting numbers of partitions (ABE-PAPER-2022-VA-GLUE) |
| `s`, `ℓ` | Circuit size and attribute length (ABE-PAPER-2026-LZF-CONSTANT-CT) |
| `N`, `D`, `B` | Arithmetic-circuit size, depth, and lookup-table base (ABE-PAPER-2025-SB-LUT) |
| `Q`, `x` | Automaton state set and input string; sizes use their cardinality and length (ABE-PAPER-2019-AMY-DFA) |
| `λ`, `lam` | Security parameter |1:["$","$L6",null,{"catalog":{"catalogVersion":"abe-landscape-v2-atomic-contributions","constructions":[{"assumption_family":"standard","assumption_id":"ASM-DBDH","assumption_ids":["ASM-DBDH"],"assumption_name":"Decisional Bilinear Diffie-Hellman (DBDH)","authors":["Vipul Goyal","Omkant Pandey","Amit Sahai","Brent Waters"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"I","id":"gpsw","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Attribute-Based Encryption for Fine-Grained Access Control of Encrypted Data","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2006/309","policy_class":"tree","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"selective","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"m","GT":"1","note":"One element \\( E_i = g_1^{t_i s} \\) per ciphertext attribute, plus \\( E' = M \\cdot Y^s \\) in G_T."},"MPK":{"G1":"U+1","GT":"1","note":"\\( T_1=g^{t_1}, \\ldots, T_U=g^{t_U} \\) plus \\( e(g,g)^y \\). U = attribute universe size. Generators not counted."},"MSK":{"Zp":"U+1","note":"t_1, ..., t_U, y"},"SK":{"G2":"n1","note":"One element \\( D_x = g_2^{q_x(0)/t_{att(x)}} \\) per tree leaf. No G_1 pieces."},"note_on_port":"GPSW'06 is originally in symmetric pairings; the standard Type III port places the key in G_2 and CT in G_1 (per FABEO Table 5).","sources":["GPSW §4.2 construction (natural Type III port)","FABEO Table 5 (Riepel–Wee CCS 2022) row for GPSW"]},"summary":"The first KP-ABE. Shamir-style polynomial secret sharing over a monotone access tree; ciphertext carries per-attribute exponentiations. Selective security under the standard DBDH assumption.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2006-GPSW","year":2006},{"assumption_family":"GGM+ROM","assumption_id":"ASM-GGM","assumption_ids":["ASM-GGM"],"assumption_name":"Generic bilinear group","authors":["John Bethencourt","Amit Sahai","Brent Waters"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"2*I+1","id":"bsw","large_universe":1,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Ciphertext-Policy Attribute-Based Encryption","paper_url":"https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~bwaters/publications/papers/cp-abe.pdf","policy_class":"tree","primitive":"CP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"GGM+ROM","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"n1","G2":"n1+1","GT":"1","note":"n1 C_y in G1 (\\( H(\\rho(y))^{q_y(0)} \\)), n1 C'_y in G2 plus C in G2, Ctilde in GT"},"MPK":{"G2":"2","GT":"1","note":"\\( A = g_2^{\\beta} \\), \\( B = g_1^{1/\\beta} \\) (placed per FAME port); Y_alpha in GT. Generators not counted. H in ROM."},"MSK":{"G1":"1","Zp":"1","note":"\\( (\\beta, g_1^{\\alpha}) \\)"},"SK":{"G1":"m+1","G2":"m","note":"D in G1, m D_tau in G1 (contain H(tau)), m D'_tau in G2"},"note_on_port":"BSW'07 is originally in symmetric pairings; the numbers above are the standard Type III port quoted by FAME and re-used by FABEO for comparison.","sources":["FAME (Agrawal–Chase CCS 2017) §5, Fig 5.5","FABEO (Riepel–Wee CCS 2022) Table 5 (asymmetric port)"]},"summary":"First CP-ABE. Monotone access tree with threshold gates; large universe via ROM hash. Per-attribute fresh randomness \\( r_\\tau \\) doubles SK vs. Waters11. Proven only in GGM + ROM.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2007-BSW","year":2007},{"assumption_family":"q-type","assumption_id":"ASM-QPBDHE","assumption_ids":["ASM-QPBDHE"],"assumption_name":"q-parallel BDHE","authors":["Brent Waters"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"2*I+1","id":"waters11","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Ciphertext-Policy Attribute-Based Encryption: An Expressive, Efficient, and Provably Secure Realization","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2008/290","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"CP-ABE","security_mode":"selective","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"n1","G2":"n1+1","GT":"1","note":"n1 C_i in G1, n1 D_i in G2 plus \\( C' = g_2^s \\) in G2, Ctilde in GT"},"MPK":{"G1":"U+1","G2":"1","GT":"1","note":"\\( g_1^a \\) and \\( \\{h_x\\}_{x \\in U} \\) in G1; g_2 in G2; Y_alpha in GT. Generators not counted. U = |U| = universe size."},"MSK":{"G1":"1","note":"\\( g_1^{\\alpha} \\)"},"SK":{"G1":"m+1","G2":"1","note":"\\( K = g_1^{\\alpha} \\cdot g_1^{at} \\), m \\( K_x = h_x^t \\) in G1, \\( L = g_2^t \\) in G2"},"note_on_port":"Waters11 is originally in symmetric pairings; the numbers above are the standard Type III port quoted by FAME and re-used by FABEO for comparison.","sources":["FAME (Agrawal–Chase CCS 2017) §5, Fig 5.5","FABEO (Riepel–Wee CCS 2022) Table 5 (asymmetric port)"]},"summary":"First CP-ABE for LSSS policies with polynomial-loss selective security under a q-type assumption. Reuses a single SK randomness t across all attributes, giving SK = (m+1) G1 + 1 G2 at the cost of a one-use restriction.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2011-WATERS","year":2011},{"assumption_family":"standard","assumption_id":"ASM-COMPOSITE-SD","assumption_ids":["ASM-COMPOSITE-SD"],"assumption_name":"Static assumptions 1–4 of LW11 (subgroup decision and a BDH-type assumption)","authors":["Allison Lewko","Brent Waters"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"4*I","id":"lw11_kpabe","large_universe":1,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Unbounded HIBE and Attribute-Based Encryption","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2011/049","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"selective","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"3*m+1","GT":"1","note":"3 elements per CT attribute (\\( C_{s_i,1}, C_{s_i,2}, C_{s_i,3} \\)) plus \\( C_0 = g^s \\). Plus \\( C = M \\cdot e(g,g)^{\\alpha s} \\) in GT."},"MPK":{"G1":"7","GT":"1","note":"\\( g, u, h, v, w \\in G_{p_1} \\) plus \\( e(g,g)^{\\alpha} \\). All in the composite-order group; the G_1 tag here labels the one source group used."},"MSK":{"Zp":"1","note":"alpha"},"SK":{"G2":"4*n1","note":"4 elements per LSSS row: K_{x,0}, K_{x,1}, K_{x,2}, K_{x,3}. Placed here under G_2 for the Type III analogue; in the paper all in one composite-order group."},"note_on_port":"Composite-order N = p_1 p_2 p_3 groups in the original paper. Size row below is in the single composite-order group G; no prime-order port is given in this paper (later work by Okamoto–Takashima, CGKW18 provide prime-order unbounded ABE).","sources":["LW11 §5.1 KP-ABE construction"]},"summary":"First unbounded KP-ABE: \\( O(1) \\) MPK, no a-priori attribute universe. Uses composite-order groups (\\( N = p_1 p_2 p_3 \\)) and nested dual-system encryption to hide ephemeral semi-functionality across key queries.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2011-LW-UNBOUNDED","year":2011},{"assumption_family":"q-type","assumption_id":"ASM-RW13-Q1","assumption_ids":["ASM-RW13-Q1"],"assumption_name":"Rouselakis–Waters q-1 assumption (q-1)","authors":["Yannis Rouselakis","Brent Waters"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"3*I+1","id":"rw13_cpabe","large_universe":1,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Practical Constructions and New Proof Methods for Large Universe Attribute-Based Encryption","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2012/583","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"CP-ABE","security_mode":"selective","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"2*n1+1","G2":"n1+1","GT":"1","note":"C_0 plus 2 per row (C_{i,1}, C_{i,2}) in G1; 1 per row (C_{i,3}) plus one master in G2; Ctilde in GT"},"MPK":{"G1":"4","GT":"1","note":"u, h, w, v in G1; Y_alpha in GT. Generators not counted."},"MSK":{"Zp":"1","note":"just alpha"},"SK":{"G1":"2*m+2","G2":"m+1","note":"K_0 and m K_{tau,3} in G1; K_1 and m K_{tau,2} in G2 (asymmetric port)"},"note_on_port":"RW13 states the scheme in symmetric pairings; the paper's own Table 2 (Charm benchmark) and FAME's Fig 5.5 both give the Type III element counts above.","sources":["RW13 (Rouselakis–Waters CCS 2013) §4.1 construction + Table 2 benchmark (element counts given as asymmetric port for Charm)","FAME (Agrawal–Chase CCS 2017) §5 (cross-checks the Type III port)"]},"summary":"First practical unbounded-universe CP-ABE without ROM. Replaces Waters11's per-attribute \\( \\{h_x\\} \\) with a two-element universe encoder (u, h) that maps any \\( \\tau \\in \\mathbb{Z}_p \\) to \\( u^{\\tau} \\cdot h \\). Pays factor-2 blowup in SK and 3× per-row CT for large universe + one-use LSSS + selective security under q-type.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2013-RW","year":2013},{"assumption_family":"LWE","assumption_id":"ASM-LWE","assumption_ids":["ASM-LWE"],"assumption_name":"Learning With Errors (LWE)","authors":["Dan Boneh","Valeria Nikolaenko","Gil Segev"],"capabilities":["arithmetic-circuits","unbounded-fan-in","key-delegation","post-quantum"],"construction_family":"lattice","decrypt_cost":{"note":"Evaluation work scales with the arithmetic circuit; correctness is controlled by depth-dependent noise growth.","pairings":"0","primary":"key-homomorphic circuit evaluation + lattice decryption"},"decrypt_pairings":"0","id":"bns13_arithmetic_kpabe","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Attribute-Based Encryption for Arithmetic Circuits","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2013/669","policy_class":"arithmetic_circuit","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"selective","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"asymptotic":"\\( O(L \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda,d)) \\)","note":"Dual-Regev samples for the L-coordinate public attribute vector."},"MPK":{"asymptotic":"\\( O(L \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda,d)) \\)","note":"L public attribute matrices plus the lattice trapdoor public matrix; the hidden factor includes \\( n \\), \\( m \\) and \\( \\log q \\)."},"SK":{"asymptotic":"\\( O(d^{2}) \\)","note":"A single \\( 2m \\times m \\) low-norm matrix; unlike prior schemes, no multiplicative dependence on circuit size."},"sources":["BNS13 §1, Parameters and performance","BNS13 §3 construction"]},"summary":"Selectively secure LWE KP-ABE for polynomial-size arithmetic circuits, with a single matrix secret key whose asymptotic size depends on depth rather than circuit gate or wire count.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2013-BNS-ARITH","year":2013},{"assumption_family":"q-type","assumption_id":"ASM-QBDHE","assumption_ids":["ASM-QBDHE"],"assumption_name":"decisional q-BDHE","authors":["Susan Hohenberger","Brent Waters"],"capabilities":["fast-decryption","large-universe","user-tunable-tradeoff"],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{"exponentiations":"2I","note":"For AND/OR-derived LSSS coefficients, many exponentiations have exponent 0 or 1.","pairings":"2","primary":"2 pairings + 2I exponentiations"},"decrypt_pairings":"2","id":"hw13_fast_kpabe","large_universe":1,"multi_use_attributes":1,"paper_title":"Attribute-Based Encryption with Fast Decryption","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2013/265","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"selective","security_model":"ROM","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"m+1","GT":"1","note":"One group element per ciphertext attribute, one header, and one masked message."},"MPK":{"G1":"1","GT":"1","note":"Large-universe ROM version hashes attributes into the group; generators and the hash description are not counted."},"MSK":{"Zp":"1"},"SK":{"G1":"n1*(Gamma+1)","note":"Each LSSS row has D_i, R_i, and one helper for each other distinct policy attribute."},"sources":["HW13 §3.1–§3.3"]},"summary":"Expressive large-universe KP-ABE whose optimized path decrypts with two pairings, at the cost of a secret-key blow-up by the number of distinct attributes in the policy.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2013-HW-FAST","year":2013},{"assumption_family":"q-type","assumption_id":"ASM-RW13-Q2","assumption_ids":["ASM-RW13-Q2"],"assumption_name":"Rouselakis–Waters q-2 assumption (q-2)","authors":["Yannis Rouselakis","Brent Waters"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"3*I+1","id":"rw13_kpabe","large_universe":1,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Practical Constructions and New Proof Methods for Large Universe Attribute-Based Encryption","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2012/583","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"selective","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"2*m+1","G2":"m","GT":"1","note":"Per attribute: C_{tau,1} in G_1, C_{tau,2} in G_1, C_{tau,3} in G_2; plus C_0 in G_1 and Ctilde in G_T."},"MPK":{"G1":"4","GT":"1","note":"u, h, w, v in G_1 plus Y_alpha in G_T. Same MPK structure as RW13 CP-ABE."},"MSK":{"Zp":"1"},"SK":{"G1":"n1","G2":"2*n1+1","note":"Per LSSS row: K_{i,0} in G_2, K_{i,1} in G_1, K_{i,2} in G_2; plus K_0 in G_2. Dual of CP-ABE."},"note_on_port":"RW13 states the scheme in symmetric pairings; sizes below follow the paper's own Charm benchmark (Table 2) asymmetric port.","sources":["RW13 §4.2 KP-ABE construction","RW13 Table 2 (Charm benchmark) asymmetric element counts"]},"summary":"KP-ABE dual of the RW13 CP-ABE. Same universe encoder (u, h), same \\( O(1) \\) MPK, large-universe via \\( \\mathbb{Z}_p \\) attribute labels, no ROM. Sizes transpose: SK grows with LSSS rows, CT grows with attribute count.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2013-RW","year":2013},{"assumption_family":"q-type","assumption_id":"ABE-ASSUMPTION-Q-TYPE-COMPUTATIONAL-PES","assumption_name":"q-type (computational PES)","authors":["Nuttapong Attrapadung"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"\\( O(1) \\)","id":"attrapadung14","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Dual System Encryption via Doubly Selective Security: Framework, Fully-secure Functional Encryption for Regular Languages, and More","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2014/428","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"\\( O(1) \\)","GT":"1","note":"CONSTANT ciphertext: 3–4 group elements in G_1 + 1 G_T, independent of |S| (up to bound T)."},"MPK":{"G1":"\\( O(T) \\)","GT":"1","note":"T is the a-priori ciphertext-attribute bound; MPK linear in T."},"SK":{"G2":"n1*T","note":"SK quadratic in policy size and ciphertext-attribute bound (short-CT KP-ABE spends SK for compact CT)."},"note_on_port":"Composite-order in the original paper; Agrawal–Chase (TCC 2016-A, FAME CCS 2017) and Chen–Gay–Wee port to prime-order. Sizes below are for the headline **short-CT** KP-ABE with ciphertext-attribute bound T.","sources":["Attrapadung Eurocrypt 2014 §6 (PES instantiations)","Agrawal–Chase TCC 2016-A 'Study of Pair Encodings' comparison tables"]},"summary":"Introduces pair encoding schemes (PES): a combinatorial object that compiles generically to adaptive ABE via dual-system encryption. This spec captures the headline \"short-CT\" KP-ABE instantiation; other instantiations (short-SK, regular languages, unbounded) also exist.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2014-ATTRAPADUNG","year":2014},{"assumption_family":"standard","assumption_id":"ASM-LWE","assumption_ids":["ASM-LWE"],"assumption_name":"Learning With Errors (LWE)","authors":["Dan Boneh","Craig Gentry","Sergey Gorbunov","Shai Halevi","Valeria Nikolaenko","Gil Segev","Vinod Vaikuntanathan","Dhinakaran Vinayagamurthy"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"lattice","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"0","id":"bgg14_kpabe","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Fully Key-Homomorphic Encryption, Arithmetic Circuit ABE, and Compact Garbled Circuits","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2014/356","policy_class":"circuit","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"selective","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"note":"L LWE samples + 1 message-carrying LWE sample. \\( \\mathrm{poly}(L, \\lambda, d) \\)."},"MPK":{"note":"L lattice matrices each of dim \\( O(n \\cdot \\log q) \\), where L = attribute bit-length and n = LWE dim. \\( \\mathrm{poly}(L, \\lambda, d) \\)."},"SK":{"note":"Short lattice vector of dim \\( m_{total} = O(L \\cdot m) \\). Length scales as \\( \\mathrm{poly}(d, \\lambda) \\); INDEPENDENT of circuit size."},"sources":["BGG+14 §4 construction","BGG+14 Theorem 4 size statement"]},"summary":"First KP-ABE for circuits from LWE. Introduces the BGG+ key-homomorphic evaluation primitive that downstream lattice ABE schemes (GVW13 KP, BV16, Tsabary19, LLL22, HLL24, Wee24, Wee25) all build on.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2014-BGGPS","year":2014},{"assumption_family":"standard","assumption_id":"ASM-KLIN","assumption_ids":["ASM-KLIN"],"assumption_name":"k-Lin (MDDH_k, Escala et al.)","authors":["Jie Chen","Romain Gay","Hoeteck Wee"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"2*(k+1)","id":"cgw15_cpabe","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Improved Dual System ABE in Prime-Order Groups via Predicate Encodings","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2015/409","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"CP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"2*U+2","GT":"1","note":"SXDH (k=1). General k-Lin: (k+1)(n_1+1). Here n_1 = U due to one-use."},"MPK":{"G1":"2*U+3","GT":"1","note":"SXDH (k=1) instantiation. General k-Lin: k(k+1)(U+1)+k in G1, k in GT. U = universe size."},"SK":{"G2":"2*U+4","note":"SXDH (k=1). General k-Lin: (k+1)(U+2). SK grows with universe U, not |S|, under one-use restriction."},"sources":["CGW15 Fig. 4 (p. 6): general k-Lin and SXDH (k=1) / DLIN (k=2) instantiations","CGW15 Appendix B.2: explicit CP-ABE scheme"]},"summary":"First fully adaptive CP-ABE in prime-order groups under a static k-Lin assumption, via the predicate-encoding framework. Achieves dual-system security without composite-order groups, at \\( (k+1)\\times \\) blowup vs. selective.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2015-CGW","year":2015},{"assumption_family":"standard","assumption_id":"ASM-KLIN","assumption_ids":["ASM-KLIN"],"assumption_name":"k-Lin (MDDH_k, Escala et al.)","authors":["Jie Chen","Romain Gay","Hoeteck Wee"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"2*(k+1)","id":"cgw15_kpabe","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Improved Dual System ABE in Prime-Order Groups via Predicate Encodings","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2015/409","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"2*U+2","GT":"1","note":"SXDH (k=1). General k-Lin: (k+1)(U+1). Under one-use, U acts like max CT-attribute count."},"MPK":{"G1":"2*U+3","GT":"1","note":"SXDH (k=1) instantiation; general k-Lin: k(k+1)(U+1)+k in G_1, k in GT."},"SK":{"G2":"2*n1+2","note":"SXDH (k=1). General k-Lin: (k+1)(n_1+1). Dual of CP-ABE: SK grows with LSSS rows."},"sources":["CGW15 Fig. 4 (p. 6) k-Lin / SXDH / DLIN instantiations","CGW15 §B.1 explicit KP-ABE scheme"]},"summary":"KP-ABE dual of CGW15 CP-ABE. First adaptive KP-ABE from static k-Lin in prime-order groups via predicate encodings. Fixed 2(k+1) pairings.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2015-CGW","year":2015},{"assumption_family":"GGM","assumption_id":"ASM-GGM","assumption_ids":["ASM-GGM"],"assumption_name":"Generic bilinear group","authors":["Miguel Ambrona","Gilles Barthe","Romain Gay","Hoeteck Wee"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"I","id":"abgw17_cpabe","large_universe":1,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Attribute-Based Encryption in the Generic Group Model: Automated Proofs and New Constructions","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/983","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"CP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"GGM","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"3*n1","GT":"1","note":"3 G_1 elements per LSSS row. Uses \\( W^{\\lambda_i} v^{t_i}, (u^{\\rho(i)} h)^{-t_i}, g^{t_i} \\) structure."},"MPK":{"G1":"\\( O(1) \\)","note":"Constant number of G_1 elements + 1 G_T. No per-attribute public material (uses rational 1/b encoding instead)."},"SK":{"G2":"m+2","note":"Uses rational encoding x_i * r / b_i per attribute plus (a-r) head. All in G_2."},"abstraction_note":"The paper's concrete scheme has 3 G_1 elements per LSSS row (a\n\\( W^{\\lambda_i} v^{t_i}, (u^{\\rho(i)} h)^{-t_i}, g^{t_i} \\) triple) and stores SK\nentries only for attributes in S (hence m+2). The PES construction\nblock below collapses these to one vector `c` of size N = n_1 and\na vector `k2` of size N (with x-gating that zeros out non-S entries),\nso the construction-block count differs from the paper sizes.\n","sources":["ABGW17 §5 CP-ABE scheme","FABEO Table 5 (Riepel–Wee CCS 2022) for the comparison row","verifiers/ggm-symbolic-solver/examples/cp_abe.ggm (used to verify in this repo)"]},"summary":"Large-universe adaptive CP-ABE with automated GGM proof via a Master Theorem; the direct basis of ggm-symbolic-solver. Rational 1/b encoding in SK quarantines Type I self-pairings, making the scheme Type I-safe.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2017-ABGW","year":2017},{"assumption_family":"standard","assumption_id":"ASM-DLIN","assumption_ids":["ASM-DLIN"],"assumption_name":"Decisional Linear (DLIN, Type III)","authors":["Shashank Agrawal","Melissa Chase"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"6","id":"fame_cpabe","large_universe":1,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"FAME: Fast Attribute-Based Message Encryption","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/807","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"CP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"ROM","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"3*n1","G2":"3","GT":"1","note":"3 ct_0 in G_2; per row 3 elements in G_1; 1 ct' in G_T."},"MPK":{"G2":"3","GT":"2","note":"h, H_1, H_2 in G_2; T_1, T_2 in G_T. Hash H in ROM, not counted."},"MSK":{"G1":"6","G2":"1","Zp":"4","note":"\\( (g, h, a_1, a_2, b_1, b_2, g^{d_1}, g^{d_2}, g^{d_3}) \\)"},"SK":{"G1":"3*m+3","G2":"3","note":"3 sk_0 in G_2; per attribute 3 elements in G_1 \\( (sk_{y,1}, sk_{y,2}, g^{-\\sigma_y}) \\); 3 sk' in G_1. Multi-use tau_hat duplicates attribute labels: (3*tau_hat*m+3) |G_1|."},"sources":["FAME (Agrawal–Chase CCS 2017) Figure 3.1 construction","FAME Fig 5.4 (decryption pairings)"]},"summary":"First fast, adaptive, unbounded CP-ABE from a standard (DLIN) assumption with a fixed 6-pairing decryption. Became the Charm-framework baseline. Tradeoff: each key/CT row spends 3 \\( \\mathbb{G}_1 \\) elements to support the DLIN proof.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2017-FAME","year":2017},{"assumption_family":"GGM","assumption_id":"ASM-GGM","assumption_ids":["ASM-GGM"],"assumption_name":"Generic bilinear group","authors":["Miguel Ambrona","Gilles Barthe","Romain Gay","Hoeteck Wee"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"I","id":"abgw17_kpabe","large_universe":1,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Attribute-Based Encryption in the Generic Group Model: Automated Proofs and New Constructions","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/983","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"GGM","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"2*m","GT":"1","note":"2 elements per CT attribute in G_1, plus 1 G_T for message blinder."},"MPK":{"G1":"\\( O(1) \\)","note":"Constant; same universe-encoder approach as RW13."},"SK":{"G2":"2*n1","note":"2 elements per LSSS row in G_2."},"abstraction_note":"Declared sizes are from the paper (2 elements per LSSS row, 2 per\nCT attribute). The PES construction block below collapses per-row\nentries into a single vector `k` of size N, so counted sizes will\ndiffer from declared sizes.\n","sources":["ABGW17 §5 KP-ABE scheme","FABEO Table 5 for the comparison row","verifiers/ggm-symbolic-solver/examples/kp_abe.ggm"]},"summary":"KP-ABE dual of ABGW17 CP-ABE: large-universe adaptive KP-ABE with automated GGM proof via the Master Theorem. Rational 1/b encoding makes it Type-I-safe.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2017-ABGW","year":2017},{"assumption_family":"standard","assumption_id":"ASM-DLIN","assumption_ids":["ASM-DLIN"],"assumption_name":"Decisional Linear (DLIN, Type III)","authors":["Shashank Agrawal","Melissa Chase"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"6","id":"fame_kpabe","large_universe":1,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"FAME: Fast Attribute-Based Message Encryption","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/807","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"ROM","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"3*m","G2":"3","GT":"1","note":"3 elements per attribute in G_1, plus 3 ct_0 in G_2, plus 1 GT. Dual of CP-ABE SK."},"MPK":{"G2":"3","GT":"2","note":"Same MPK structure as FAME CP-ABE."},"SK":{"G1":"3*n1","G2":"3","note":"3 elements per LSSS row in G_1, plus 3 sk_0 in G_2. Dual of CP-ABE CT."},"sources":["FAME §4 KP-ABE construction","FAME Fig 5.5 size table"]},"summary":"KP-ABE dual of FAME CP-ABE. Adaptive DLIN security with fixed 6-pairing decryption. Large-universe via ROM-modeled hash. Sizes transpose from the CP-ABE variant.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2017-FAME","year":2017},{"assumption_family":"standard","assumption_id":"ASM-KLIN","assumption_ids":["ASM-KLIN"],"assumption_name":"k-Lin (MDDH_k)","authors":["Jie Chen","Junqing Gong","Lucas Kowalczyk","Hoeteck Wee"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"(2*k+1)*I","id":"cgkw18","large_universe":1,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Unbounded ABE via Bilinear Entropy Expansion, Revisited","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/116","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"(2*k+1)*m+k","GT":"1","note":"Per CT attribute: 2k+1 elements in G_1, plus k-size header and GT mask. At SXDH: 3m+1 G_1 plus GT."},"MPK":{"G1":"\\( O(1) \\)","note":"\\( O(k^2) \\) scalar-level but constant in universe / policy size. The headline claim is \\( O(1) \\) MPK for an unbounded scheme."},"SK":{"G2":"(5*k+3)*n1","note":"Per row: 2k+1 + k+1 + 2k+1 = 5k+3 elements in G_2. At k=1 (SXDH): 8*n_1."},"sources":["CGKW18 §4 prime-order KP-ABE construction"]},"summary":"First adaptive unbounded KP-ABE from standard k-Lin in Type-III prime- order groups. Bilinear entropy expansion lemma shrinks vector height from 3k (OT12) to 2k+1, improving all follow-up unbounded schemes.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2018-CGKW","year":2018},{"assumption_family":"standard","assumption_id":"ASM-DLIN","assumption_ids":["ASM-DLIN"],"assumption_name":"DLIN","authors":["Shweta Agrawal","Monosij Maitra","Shota Yamada"],"capabilities":["dfa","uniform-computation","unbounded-input","unbounded-machine","unbounded-key-queries"],"construction_family":"compiler","decrypt_cost":{"note":"The CP orientation is obtained by swapping the two underlying ABE roles; the source does not present a single normalized pairing count.","primary":"composed MSP reconstruction"},"decrypt_pairings":"not reported in a comparable form","id":"amy19_dfa_cpabe","large_universe":1,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Attribute Based Encryption for Deterministic Finite Automata from DLIN","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/645","policy_class":"DFA","primitive":"CP-ABE","security_mode":"selective","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"asymptotic":"\\( O(|Q|^{2} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\)","note":"Ciphertext carrying the DFA policy in the swapped compiler."},"MPK":{"asymptotic":"\\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\)","note":"Independent of input length and DFA size through unbounded MSP components."},"SK":{"asymptotic":"\\( O(|x|^{3} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\)","note":"Attribute key for an unbounded input string in the swapped compiler."},"sources":["AMY19 §1.2–§1.4 and the symmetric CP compiler"]},"summary":"Ciphertext-policy counterpart of the DLIN DFA compiler, supporting unbounded machines, inputs, and key requests by swapping its two MSP-ABE components.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2019-AMY-DFA","year":2019},{"assumption_family":"standard","assumption_id":"ASM-KLIN","assumption_ids":["ASM-KLIN"],"assumption_name":"k-Lin (\\( \\mathrm{MDDH}_k^1 \\))","authors":["Lucas Kowalczyk","Hoeteck Wee"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"2*I+1","id":"kw19_cpabe","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Compact Adaptively Secure ABE for NC1 from k-Lin","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/224","policy_class":"NC1","primitive":"CP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"(k+1)*(2*n1+1)","GT":"1","note":"1 + 2n_1 vectors in G_1 of length k+1, plus 1 G_T. Grows with policy size n_1."},"MPK":{"G1":"k*(k+1)*(U+1)+k","GT":"1","note":"n+2 matrices in G_1 of size k x (k+1), plus 1 G_T element. U = attribute universe size."},"SK":{"G2":"(k+1)*(m+2)","note":"m+2 vectors in G_2 of length k+1. |S|-dependent, policy-size-INDEPENDENT. Compactness axis."},"sources":["KW19 Appendix A.1 CP-ABE construction","KW19 §1.1 informal parameter claims"]},"summary":"First adaptive ABE for NC1 (monotone Boolean formulas) from static k-Lin in prime-order groups with attribute multi-use and polynomial security loss. Secret key is policy-size-independent (\"compact\").","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2019-KW","year":2019},{"assumption_family":"standard","assumption_id":"ASM-DLIN","assumption_ids":["ASM-DLIN"],"assumption_name":"DLIN","authors":["Shweta Agrawal","Monosij Maitra","Shota Yamada"],"capabilities":["dfa","uniform-computation","unbounded-input","unbounded-machine","unbounded-key-queries"],"construction_family":"compiler","decrypt_cost":{"note":"The paper's headline comparison reports total key/ciphertext asymptotics rather than one normalized pairing formula.","primary":"composed MSP reconstruction"},"decrypt_pairings":"not reported in a comparable form","id":"amy19_dfa_kpabe","large_universe":1,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Attribute Based Encryption for Deterministic Finite Automata from DLIN","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/645","policy_class":"DFA","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"selective","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"asymptotic":"\\( O(|x|^{3} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\)","note":"Ciphertext for an input string x."},"MPK":{"asymptotic":"\\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\)","note":"Independent of input length and DFA size through unbounded MSP components."},"SK":{"asymptotic":"\\( O(|Q|^{2} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda)) \\)","note":"Policy key for a DFA with state set Q."},"sources":["AMY19 §1.4 comparison with concurrent work"]},"summary":"First pairing-based KP-ABE for DFA from static DLIN supporting unbounded input length, machine size, and key requests; obtained through a modular MSP compiler.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2019-AMY-DFA","year":2019},{"assumption_family":"standard","assumption_id":"ASM-KLIN","assumption_ids":["ASM-KLIN"],"assumption_name":"k-Lin (\\( \\mathrm{MDDH}_k^1 \\))","authors":["Lucas Kowalczyk","Hoeteck Wee"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"2*I+1","id":"kw19_kpabe","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Compact Adaptively Secure ABE for NC1 from k-Lin","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/224","policy_class":"NC1","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"(k+1)*(U+2)","GT":"1","note":"COMPACT: \\( O(k \\cdot n) \\) CT where n = attribute length. Policy-size INDEPENDENT even in many-use."},"MPK":{"G1":"k*(U+1)*(k+1)+k","GT":"1","note":"Matrix encodings scale as k(k+1) per universe slot + const."},"SK":{"G2":"(k+1)*(2*m_formula+1)","note":"Linear in formula size m_formula = number of shares. Policy in key; grows with formula."},"sources":["KW19 §6 main KP-ABE construction","KW19 §1.1 parameter claims"]},"summary":"First compact + adaptive + many-use + k-Lin KP-ABE for NC1. CT is policy-size independent — a milestone that resolves the Lewko–Waters'11 open problem. Built from a piecewise-guessing security framework plus a refined NC1 secret-sharing scheme.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2019-KW","year":2019},{"assumption_family":"standard","assumption_id":"ASM-KLIN","assumption_ids":["ASM-KLIN"],"assumption_name":"k-Lin (MDDH_k)","authors":["Huijia Lin","Ji Luo"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"3*k+4","id":"ll20b_cpabe","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Succinct and Adaptively Secure ABE for ABP from k-Lin","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/1139","policy_class":"ABP","primitive":"CP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"(k+1)*mABP*(U+2)+mABP+k+1","GT":"1","note":"Grows with mABP = ABP size (vertex count). Under SXDH: ~m_ABP*(U+2) + m_ABP + 2 G_1 elements."},"MPK":{"G1":"k*(k+1)*(U+4)+k","GT":"1","note":"U = attribute-length parameter n in paper. k-Lin parameterized; under SXDH (k=1), approx (n+4) G_1 elements."},"SK":{"G1":"3*k+4","note":"CONSTANT in policy size. Under SXDH (k=1): 7 G_1 elements. Headline result."},"sources":["LL20b Construction 37 (Section 6.2) CP-ABE for ABP","LL20b Table 2 exact element counts"]},"summary":"First ABE with CONSTANT-size secret key under a static standard assumption (k-Lin), adaptive security, prime-order groups. SK = 3k+4 group elements (7 under SXDH), independent of ABP policy size.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2020-LL","year":2020},{"assumption_family":"standard","assumption_id":"ASM-KLIN","assumption_ids":["ASM-KLIN"],"assumption_name":"k-Lin (MDDH_k)","authors":["Huijia Lin","Ji Luo"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"\\( O(k \\cdot U \\cdot \\mathit{mABP}) \\)","id":"ll20a_kpabe","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Compact Adaptively Secure ABE from k-Lin: Beyond NC1 and towards NL","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/318","policy_class":"ABP","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"\\( O(k \\cdot U) \\)","GT":"1","note":"Single IPFE slotted ciphertext carrying (pad, \\( \\mathrm{const}^t, \\mathrm{coef}_i^t \\)) slots. Policy-size INDEPENDENT."},"MPK":{"G1":"\\( O(k \\cdot U) \\)","note":"\\( \\Theta(kn) \\) IPFE public slots (const, coef_i for t in [k], i in [n])."},"SK":{"G1":"\\( O(k \\cdot U \\cdot \\mathit{mABP}) \\)","note":"(mABP+1) IPFE secret keys, each with \\( O(kn) \\) group elements. Linear in ABP size — improvement from quadratic in prior work [IW14, CGKW18]."},"sources":["LL20a Construction 26 (§6.3) KP-ABE for ABPs","LL20a Theorem 27 adaptive security"]},"summary":"First compact adaptive KP-ABE for arithmetic branching programs (beyond NC1) from standard k-Lin. CT size is independent of ABP size; SK grows linearly in ABP size (improvement over prior \\( O(|ABP|^2) \\) constructions).","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2020-LL-EUROCRYPT","year":2020},{"assumption_family":"standard","assumption_id":"ASM-KLIN","assumption_ids":["ASM-KLIN"],"assumption_name":"k-Lin (MDDH_k)","authors":["Huijia Lin","Ji Luo"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"2*k+3","id":"ll20b_kpabe","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Succinct and Adaptively Secure ABE for ABP from k-Lin","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/1139","policy_class":"ABP","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"2*k+3","GT":"1","note":"CONSTANT — 5 group elements under SXDH (k=1), 2k+3 in general. Headline result."},"MPK":{"G2":"\\( O(k \\cdot U) \\)","note":"Public IPFE slots + gradually-sim-secure machinery."},"SK":{"G2":"\\( O(k \\cdot U \\cdot \\mathit{mABP}) \\)","note":"Grows with ABP size."},"sources":["LL20b §5 KP-ABE Construction 32","LL20b Table 2 exact element counts"]},"summary":"First KP-ABE with CONSTANT-size ciphertexts for a rich policy class (ABP), adaptive under static k-Lin. CT = 5 group elements at SXDH. Breaks the long-standing \"succinct implies selective or q-type\" pattern.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2020-LL","year":2020},{"assumption_family":"GGM+ROM","assumption_id":"ASM-GGM","assumption_ids":["ASM-GGM"],"assumption_name":"Generic bilinear group","authors":["Doreen Riepel","Hoeteck Wee"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"tau+2","id":"fabeo_cpabe","large_universe":1,"multi_use_attributes":1,"paper_title":"FABEO: Fast Attribute-Based Encryption with Optimal Security","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/1415","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"CP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"GGM+ROM","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"n1","G2":"tau+1","GT":"1","note":"n_1 row elements in G_1; (tau+1) in G_2 carrying shared randomness across multi-use reuse."},"MPK":{"G1":"1","G2":"1","GT":"1","note":"\\( g_1^{\\alpha} \\) (implicit), \\( g_2 \\), \\( e(g_1,g_2)^{\\alpha} \\), + ROM hash \\( H: \\{0,1\\}^* \\to G_1 \\). All \\( O(1) \\)."},"SK":{"G1":"m+1","G2":"1","note":"Head \\( K = g_1^{\\alpha+r \\cdot b_{|U|+1}} \\in G_1 \\), m \\( K_u = g_1^{r \\cdot b_u} \\in G_1 \\), \\( L = g_2^r \\in G_2 \\)."},"abstraction_note":"Declared sizes follow FABEO Table 5. The PES construction block\nbelow captures the τ=1 specialization with a single sp slot,\nwhich differs slightly in per-slot counting from the paper's\nparametric τ. Expected divergence in size-consistency checks.\n","sources":["FABEO §5 construction","FABEO Table 5 exact element counts"]},"summary":"Among the pairing CP-ABE schemes compared in the paper, it reports the smallest keys and ciphertexts and the fewest decryption pairings (2–3), optimal security bound \\( O(t^2/p) \\) matching the discrete-log lower bound, native attribute multi-use, large universe. Proven in GGM + ROM.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2022-FABEO","year":2022},{"assumption_family":"q-type","assumption_id":"ABE-ASSUMPTION-Q-TYPE-PAIR-ENCODING-INSTANTIATION","assumption_name":"q-type pair-encoding instantiation","authors":["Marloes Venema","Greg Alpár"],"capabilities":["unbounded","flexible-efficiency-tradeoff","non-monotone-extension","online-offline","label-reuse"],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{"exponentiations":"2I","note":"Changing nk and nc shifts work between key generation, encryption, and decryption.","pairings":"2+ceil(I/nk)+ceil(I/nc)","primary":"2 + ceil(I/nk) + ceil(I/nc) pairings"},"decrypt_pairings":"2+ceil(I/nk)+ceil(I/nc)","id":"glue22_cpabe","large_universe":1,"multi_use_attributes":1,"paper_title":"GLUE: Generalizing Unbounded Attribute-Based Encryption for Flexible Efficiency Trade-Offs","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/613","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"CP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"1+2*n1+mct","GT":"1","note":"mct = max(ceil(n1/nc), tau) ciphertext partitions."},"MPK":{"G1":"nk+2*nc","G2":"1","GT":"1","note":"Partition parameters nk and nc are selected at setup."},"MSK":{"Zp":"nk+2*nc+1"},"SK":{"G2":"2+m+mkey","note":"mkey = ceil(m/nk) key partitions."},"sources":["GLUE §4 Definition 6","GLUE Table 2"]},"summary":"Generalized, large-universe, unbounded and expressive CP-ABE whose nk/nc partition parameters expose a flexible encryption/decryption trade-off and support non-monotone and online/offline extensions.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2022-VA-GLUE","year":2022},{"assumption_family":"hybrid","assumption_id":"ASM-LWE","assumption_ids":["ASM-LWE","ASM-GGM"],"assumption_name":"LWE + Generic bilinear group (hybrid)","authors":["Hanjun Li","Huijia Lin","Ji Luo"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"hybrid","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"\\( O(1) \\)","id":"lll22_cpabe","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"ABE for Circuits with Constant-Size Secret Keys and Adaptive Security","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/659","policy_class":"NC1","primitive":"CP-ABE","security_mode":"selective","security_model":"GGM+LWE","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"\\( \\mathrm{poly}(U,\\mathit{lam}) \\)","note":"Linear in attribute length U, policy-size-INDEPENDENT. 'Double succinct' claim. U = |x| = attribute length."},"MPK":{"G1":"\\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\mathit{lam},d) \\)","note":"Lattice matrices (poly in depth d and security parameter). lam = security parameter (renamed from 'lambda' which is a Python keyword)."},"SK":{"G1":"3","note":"LITERALLY 3 GROUP ELEMENTS, independent of circuit/formula size. Hybrid lattice–pairing compression."},"sources":["Li–Lin–Luo TCC 2022 Construction 2 (CP-ABE for Boolean formulas)","Paper title + abstract: 'constant-size secret keys and adaptive security'"]},"summary":"First \"doubly succinct\" ABE: constant-size SK (3 group elements) AND policy-size-independent CT. Combines lattice (BGG+-style) attribute evaluation with pairing (GGM) compression of the short-preimage key.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2022-LLL","year":2022},{"assumption_family":"GGM+ROM","assumption_id":"ASM-GGM","assumption_ids":["ASM-GGM"],"assumption_name":"Generic bilinear group","authors":["Doreen Riepel","Hoeteck Wee"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"pairing","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"tau+1","id":"fabeo_kpabe","large_universe":1,"multi_use_attributes":1,"paper_title":"FABEO: Fast Attribute-Based Encryption with Optimal Security","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/1415","policy_class":"monotone_LSSS","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"adaptive","security_model":"GGM+ROM","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"m","G2":"1","GT":"1","note":"m attribute elements in G_1, 1 top-level G_2, 1 GT blinder."},"MPK":{"G1":"1","G2":"1","GT":"1","note":"\\( g_1, g_2, e(g_1,g_2)^{\\alpha} \\); \\( H: \\{0,1\\}^* \\to G_1 \\) in ROM. All \\( O(1) \\)."},"SK":{"G1":"n1","G2":"tau","note":"n_1 row elements in G_1; tau per-slot randomness in G_2. Dual of CP-ABE."},"sources":["FABEO §4 KP-ABE construction","FABEO Table 5 exact element counts"]},"summary":"KP-ABE dual of FABEO CP-ABE. Smallest SK and CT, \\( \\tau+1 \\) pairings (2 at \\( \\tau=1 \\)), optimal \\( O(t^{2}/p) \\) bound, native multi-use, large universe. Among the pairing KP-ABE schemes compared in the paper, it reports the smallest keys and ciphertexts.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2022-FABEO","year":2022},{"assumption_family":"hybrid","assumption_id":"ASM-LWE","assumption_ids":["ASM-LWE","ASM-GGM"],"assumption_name":"LWE + Generic bilinear group (hybrid)","authors":["Hanjun Li","Huijia Lin","Ji Luo"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"hybrid","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"\\( O(1) \\)","id":"lll22_kpabe","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"ABE for Circuits with Constant-Size Secret Keys and Adaptive Security","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/659","policy_class":"circuit","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"selective","security_model":"GGM+LWE","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"G1":"\\( \\mathrm{poly}(U,d,\\mathit{lam}) \\)","GT":"1","note":"Linear in attribute length |x| = U, polynomial in circuit depth d."},"MPK":{"G1":"\\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\mathit{lam},d) \\)","note":"Lattice trapdoor matrix + BGG+-style per-attribute matrices + \\( O(1) \\) pairing material. lam = security parameter."},"SK":{"G1":"3","note":"LITERALLY 3 GROUP ELEMENTS for any circuit of bounded depth d. Pairing-side compression of lattice short preimage."},"sources":["Li–Lin–Luo TCC 2022 Construction 1 (KP-ABE for circuits)","Paper title: 'ABE for Circuits with Constant-Size Secret Keys'"]},"summary":"First KP-ABE for BOUNDED-DEPTH CIRCUITS with constant-size (3-element) secret keys. Hybrid lattice + pairing (GGM) construction: BGG+-style circuit evaluation on LWE ciphertexts, pairing compresses the lattice short preimage into 3 group elements.","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2022-LLL","year":2022},{"assumption_family":"hybrid","assumption_id":"ASM-LWE","assumption_ids":["ASM-LWE","ASM-EVASIVE-LWE"],"assumption_name":"LWE + evasive LWE","authors":["Yao-Ching Hsieh","Huijia Lin","Ji Luo"],"capabilities":[],"construction_family":"lattice","decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"0","id":"hll24","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"A General Framework for Lattice-Based ABE Using Evasive Inner-Product Functional Encryption","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/821","policy_class":"circuit","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"selective","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"note":"§6: \\( (|C|+1) \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda,\\log M,d) \\) — linear in CIRCUIT SIZE; §8: \\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda,\\log M,d) \\) only — succinct in DEPTH; §7: \\( (|x|+1) \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\) for DFA-CP."},"MPK":{"note":"\\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda, \\log M, d) \\) for circuits; \\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\) for DFA. M is intermediate-value bound, d is depth."},"SK":{"note":"\\( (|x|+1) \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda,\\log M,d) \\) for §6 generic; constant-\\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\) for §8 succinct CP-ABE; \\( (|x|+1) \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\) for §7 DFA-CP."},"abstraction_note":"HLL24 contains MULTIPLE constructions with different size profiles.\nThe headline (eliminating tensor LWE vs Wee22) is the §8 succinct\nCP-ABE for circuits, where ct depends on circuit DEPTH only (via\nBGG+14 garbling instantiation).\n","sources":["HLL24 Corollary 21 (generic CP-ABE, §6)","HLL24 Corollary 29 (DFA, §7)","HLL24 Corollary 34 (succinct CP-ABE for circuits, §8 — sizes implied)"]},"summary":"General framework for lattice ABE: noisy linear garbling for class F + evasive IPFE → KP/CP-ABE for F. Yields succinct CP-ABE for circuits (eliminating tensor LWE), and the first lattice public-key ABE for uniform computation (DFA + logspace TM).","work_id":"ABE-PAPER-2024-HLL","year":2024},{"assumption_family":"LWE","assumption_id":"ASM-RLWE","assumption_ids":["ASM-RLWE"],"assumption_name":"Ring-LWE","authors":["Sora Suegami","Enrico Bottazzi"],"capabilities":["nonlinear-operations","lookup-tables","arithmetic-circuits","post-quantum"],"construction_family":"lattice","decrypt_cost":{"note":"Larger B reduces repeated decryption work but increases key generation and key size by a polynomial-in-B factor.","pairings":"0","primary":"\\( \\tilde O(N \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda,D)^{2} / \\log_2 B) \\) lattice evaluation"},"decrypt_pairings":"0","id":"sb25_nonlinear_kpabe","large_universe":0,"multi_use_attributes":0,"paper_title":"Lookup-Table Evaluation over Key-Homomorphic Encodings and KP-ABE for Nonlinear Operations","paper_url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/1870","policy_class":"arithmetic_circuit","primitive":"KP-ABE","security_mode":"selective","security_model":"standard","security_notion":"CPA","sizes":{"CT":{"asymptotic":"\\( \\tilde O(L \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(D) / \\log_2 B) \\)","note":"Compatible with base-B lookup-table evaluation."},"MPK":{"asymptotic":"\\( \\tilde O(L \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(D) / \\log_2 B) \\)","note":"Amortized expression; \\( \\mathrm{poly}(\\lambda) \\) and \\( \\mathrm{polylog}(\\lambda,D) \\) factors are hidden."},"SK":{"asymptotic":"\\( \\tilde O(B^{2}N \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(D) / (\\log_2 B)^{2}) \\)","note":"Decryption-key size grows polynomially in the lookup-table base B."},"sources":["SB25 Table 1"]},"summary":"Ring-LWE KP-ABE for modulo-q arithmetic circuits that evaluates nonlinear lookup tables directly over 